## Common questions

**Can I use this to train my team internally?**
Yes. Internal training at a company is not a commercial use of the *material* in the sense the license restricts — you are not selling or licensing the book. Running the labs as an internal exercise, printing the quick reference for your SOC, and adapting a lab into a tabletop scenario are all fine. Keep the attribution.

**Can I teach a paid course from it?**
Not without permission. Charging for instruction built on this material is a commercial use. Ask.

**Can I sell it, or a derivative of it?**
No, not under this license. Ask if you want different terms.

**Can I quote it in a blog post, talk, or paper?**
Yes, with attribution. Short quotations for commentary or criticism are likely fair use / fair dealing in most jurisdictions regardless, but attribution is expected either way.

**Can I fix an error and publish the corrected version?**
Yes — that is a derivative, so publish it under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 with attribution and a note of what changed. Better still, send the fix back via a pull request; see [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md).

**I want to use it commercially.**
Open an issue and ask. The NonCommercial term exists to prevent the book being resold out from under it, not to block every commercial context.

---

## A note on the simulated data

Everything under `Data/` is **fabricated**. Hostnames, IP addresses, user accounts, file hashes, and timestamps are synthetic and were constructed to serve the lab scenarios. Nothing in this repository is derived from a real incident, a real organization, or any real person's data.

Do not use these datasets to benchmark tools, validate detection logic, or train models — they mirror the *shape* of real parser output without its statistical properties, and conclusions drawn from them will not transfer.
